## Stability comment

Perturbation sensitivity is the relative change ||x_new - x_ref|| / ||x_ref|| when the regularisation parameter is perturbed.  Tikhonov uses a continuous +/-20% change in alpha; TSVD and CGLS use the natural +/-1 step in their integer regularisation parameter.

## Recommendation

Selected method: **CGLS (Morozov)**

Written to `results/solution/method_c/` and its alias folder (the mirroring uses a single in-memory source of truth, so the two folders never drift).

The selection rule is the *lowest normalised sum* across the three criteria (each divided by the smallest value across methods, then summed; smaller is better).  Tie-break by lowest rank-sum.

| Criterion | Method A (Tikhonov) | Method B (TSVD) | Method C (CGLS) |
|---|---|---|---|
| |residual - ||noise||| | 0.0757 | 0.0015 | 0.0005 |
| ||x|| | 2.6939 | 10.0414 | 8.0272 |
| max(sensitivity) | 5.715e-02 | 6.199e-01 | 5.892e-02 |
| normalised sum | 157.928 | 17.731 | 5.011 |
| rank-sum | 3 | 1 | 3 |

**Pareto dominance check.**  A method *dominates* another if it is no worse on every criterion *and* strictly better on at least one (lower is better for all three criteria):

- A dominates B: no
- A dominates C: no
- B dominates A: no
- B dominates C: no
- C dominates A: no
- C dominates B: YES

Pareto-optimal methods (not dominated by any other):
- Tikhonov (L-curve), CGLS (Morozov)

Numerical justification for `winner`:

- closest residual to noise norm: |residual - ||noise||| = 0.0005  (noise norm = 0.4000)
- solution norm: ||x|| = 8.0272
- perturbation sensitivity (max of +/- step): 5.892e-02
- normalised sum (smaller better): 5.011

The selected method gives the lowest normalised sum, i.e. the best balanced trade-off between fitting the noisy data to its noise floor, keeping the solution parsimonious (low ||x||), and being stable to small changes in its regularisation parameter.
